    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d984">

                <pb facs="0945" n="933" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg933"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114A/356: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bern</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-01T10:00:00+01:00">March 1, 1944—10
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:14 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1231. American Interests—Japan. Legation’s 1218.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d984-f1"/>
                    Swiss Foreign Office notice dated February 28 adds following résumé Gorgé’s
                    impressions following talks with Japanese Ministry Foreign Affairs.</p>
                <p>The question “atrocities” may be considered as belonging to the past. Japanese
                    Government will reply to Department’s 274 and 275 of January 27 but principal
                    problem remaining to be settled is present and future fate of prisoners of
                    war.</p>
                <pb facs="0946" n="934" xml:id="pg934"/>
                <p>During conversations Gorgé expressed conviction that Japanese Government acting
                    in own interest allowing protecting power exercise proper function inspection
                    camps and recommend improvements. He pointed out American Government in strong
                    position this connection and Japanese Government created difficult situation by
                    refusal allow Swiss visit camps Japan which gave rise to suspicions Allied
                    countries. Gorgé emphasized article 86 Prisoners of War Convention essential and
                    expressed hope Japanese Minister of War would decide observe it more liberally.
                    Also insisted delivery those prisoners of war lists which not yet delivered
                    Swiss.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
